      "text": "To find the distance between the foci of the ellipse, we need to determine the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) from the given ellipse. The standard form of the ellipse equation is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\n\nFrom the given ellipse, we observe that the lengths of the semi-major and semi-minor axes, \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), are 2 and 6, respectively. Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\na = 2 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b = 6\n\\]\n\nThe distance of the foci from the center of the ellipse is given by \\(c\\), where \\(c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\\). Substituting the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{2^2 - 6^2} = \\sqrt{4 - 36} = \\sqrt{-32}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(c\\) must be a positive value, we take the absolute value:\n\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{32} = 4\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThe distance between the foci is \\(2c\\):\n\n\\[\n2c = 2 \\times 4\\sqrt{2} = 8\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the distance between the foci is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{8\\sqrt{2}}\n\\]",
