      "text": "To find the distance between the foci of the ellipse, we need to determine the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) and use the relationship between these values and the distance between the foci. The standard form of the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\n\nFor the given ellipse, the major axis is along the y-axis because the lengths of the semi-major and semi-minor axes are 2 and 6, respectively. Therefore, \\(a = 6\\) and \\(b = 2\\).\n\nThe distance between the foci of an ellipse is given by \\(2c\\), where \\(c\\) is defined by the equation:\n\n\\[\nc^2 = a^2 - b^2\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\n\\[\nc^2 = 6^2 - 2^2 = 36 - 4 = 32\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{32} = 4\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThe distance between the foci is:\n\n\\[\n2c = 2 \\times 4\\sqrt{2} = 8\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance between the foci is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{8\\sqrt{2}}\n\\]",
